---
import Code from "#/components/Code.astro";
import Docs from "#/layouts/Docs.astro";
---

<Docs
	page="editors"
	title="Editors"
	lede="Format on save: squill as your editor's SQL formatter, and for the SQL inside your other code."
>
	<section>
		<h2>Editor integration</h2>
		<p>
			Editors hand a formatter the buffer on stdin and read the result back from
			stdout. Tell squill which file the buffer is with{" "}
			<code>--stdin-filepath</code>, and it formats it exactly as{" "}
			<code>squill fmt</code> would format that file on disk: the nearest{" "}
			<code>squill.toml</code> applies, and so do its{" "}
			<a href="docs/configuration/#rules">rules</a>, so a Rust buffer gets its
			embedded SQL formatted and a <code>*.sql.sqlite</code> file its dialect.
			The file doesn&rsquo;t need to exist yet.
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="sh"
			code={`squill fmt --stdin-filepath src/db.rs < src/db.rs`}
		/>
		<p>
			A buffer squill wouldn&rsquo;t format as a file comes back unchanged: an{" "}
			<code>ignore</code>d or <a href="docs/frozen/">frozen</a> path, or a
			host-language file no <code>[[embedded]]</code> rule covers. So it is safe
			to turn on for every file of a language, in every project.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>VS Code</h2>
		<p>
			Install{" "}
			<a href="https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=aslilac.squill">
				squill from the Visual Studio Marketplace
			</a>
			, or search for squill in the Extensions view, or from a terminal:
		</p>
		<Code lang="sh" code={`code --install-extension aslilac.squill`} />
		<p>
			The extension runs squill&rsquo;s language server: it formats SQL files
			and the SQL embedded in other languages, and highlights the embedded SQL.
			It uses the <code>squill</code> on your <code>PATH</code>, so the editor
			formats exactly as your CLI and CI do, or else downloads the latest
			release. Its{" "}
			<a href="https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=aslilac.squill">
				Marketplace page
			</a>{" "}
			covers the rest of its settings.
		</p>
		<p>
			In <code>settings.json</code>, make squill the SQL formatter, and format
			on save:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="jsonc"
			file=".vscode/settings.json"
			code={`{
  "[sql]": {
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "aslilac.squill",
    "editor.formatOnSave": true
  }
}`}
		/>
		<p>
			VS Code runs one formatter per file, so in another language that
			language&rsquo;s own formatter keeps the job. squill formats the SQL
			embedded there with a <code>source.formatSql</code> code action instead,
			which runs on save after the formatter. Turn it on for each language whose
			SQL a <code>[[embedded]]</code> rule covers (the extension offers to, when
			it finds such a rule without it):
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="jsonc"
			file=".vscode/settings.json"
			code={`{
  "[rust]": {
    "editor.codeActionsOnSave": {
      "source.formatSql": "explicit"
    }
  }
}`}
		/>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Zed</h2>
		<p>
			Install{" "}
			<a href="https://zed.dev/extensions/squill-language-server">
				squill&rsquo;s Zed extension
			</a>
			, or run <strong>zed: extensions</strong> and search for squill. Syntax
			support for SQL files comes from Zed&rsquo;s SQL extension.
		</p>
		<p>
			The extension runs squill&rsquo;s language server for SQL files and for
			the languages squill finds SQL in: it formats both, highlights the
			embedded SQL, and reports what squill leaves alone. It uses the{" "}
			<code>squill</code> on your <code>PATH</code>, or else downloads the
			latest release.
		</p>
		<p>
			Then in <code>settings.json</code>, format SQL files with squill&rsquo;s
			language server:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="jsonc"
			file=".zed/settings.json"
			code={`{
  "languages": {
    "SQL": {
      "formatter": { "language_server": { "name": "squill" } },
      "format_on_save": "on"
    }
  }
}`}
		/>
		<p>
			Zed runs a list of formatters in order, so for SQL embedded in another
			language, keep its language server&rsquo;s formatting and follow it with
			squill&rsquo;s. squill highlights the embedded SQL too, as semantic
			tokens, which Zed only asks for when <code>semantic_tokens</code> is on:{" "}
			<code>"combined"</code> lays them over Zed&rsquo;s own highlighting (
			<code>"full"</code> would replace it, leaving only the SQL colored).
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="jsonc"
			file=".zed/settings.json"
			code={`{
  "languages": {
    "Rust": {
      "formatter": [
        { "language_server": { "name": "rust-analyzer" } },
        { "language_server": { "name": "squill" } }
      ],
      "semantic_tokens": "combined"
    }
  }
}`}
		/>
		<h3>Without the extension</h3>
		<p>
			squill works as an external formatter too, with{" "}
			<code>&lbrace;buffer_path&rbrace;</code> passing the buffer&rsquo;s path
			so its config and rules apply. For SQL files:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="jsonc"
			file=".zed/settings.json"
			code={`{
  "languages": {
    "SQL": {
      "formatter": {
        "external": {
          "command": "squill",
          "arguments": ["fmt", "--stdin-filepath", "{buffer_path}"]
        }
      }
    }
  }
}`}
		/>
		<p>
			For embedded SQL, put the same <code>external</code> entry after the
			language server in that language&rsquo;s list of formatters.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Language server</h2>
		<p>
			<code>squill language-server start</code> runs a language server on stdin
			and stdout, for any editor that speaks LSP. It resolves each document by
			its path, as <code>--stdin-filepath</code> does, and offers:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				formatting, for a SQL file and for a host file&rsquo;s embedded SQL;
			</li>
			<li>
				warnings where squill leaves something alone: statements it passed
				through verbatim, and embedded strings it declined to rewrite;
			</li>
			<li>
				highlighting for embedded SQL, as semantic tokens (SQL files it leaves
				to the editor&rsquo;s own highlighting);
			</li>
			<li>
				a <code>source.formatSql</code> code action that makes the same edit as
				formatting, for editors that run one formatter per file, so it can run
				after the language&rsquo;s own.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Config is re-read for every request, so edits to <code>squill.toml</code>{" "}
			apply without a restart. Run it for SQL and for the languages your{" "}
			<code>[[embedded]]</code> rules cover. A client that takes one
			server&rsquo;s formatting or semantic tokens per document can narrow
			squill&rsquo;s to some documents with the initialization options{" "}
			<code>formattingSelector</code> and <code>semanticTokensSelector</code>,
			each a list of document filters (<code>[]</code> for none), as the VS Code
			extension does.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Helix</h2>
		<p>
			In <code>languages.toml</code>, register squill&rsquo;s language server
			and use it for SQL files:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="toml"
			file="~/.config/helix/languages.toml"
			code={`[language-server.squill]
command = "squill"
args = ["language-server", "start"]

[[language]]
name = "sql"
language-servers = ["squill"]
auto-format = true`}
		/>
		<p>
			Helix formats a language with one formatter: the first of its language
			servers that can, unless a <code>formatter</code> command replaces them.
			So for SQL embedded in another language, keep that language&rsquo;s own
			server, add squill&rsquo;s for its warnings, and format by piping the
			language&rsquo;s formatter into squill.{" "}
			<code>%&lbrace;buffer_name&rbrace;</code> passes the buffer&rsquo;s path:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="toml"
			file="~/.config/helix/languages.toml"
			code={`[[language]]
name = "rust"
language-servers = ["rust-analyzer", { name = "squill", only-features = ["diagnostics"] }]
formatter = { command = "sh", args = ["-c", "rustfmt | squill fmt --stdin-filepath \\"$0\\"", "%{buffer_name}"] }`}
		/>
		<p>
			Helix doesn&rsquo;t draw semantic tokens, so embedded SQL isn&rsquo;t
			highlighted there.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Neovim</h2>
		<p>
			Neovim 0.11 and later set up language servers without plugins. Register
			squill for SQL and every language it finds SQL in, next to those
			languages&rsquo; own servers:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="lua"
			file="~/.config/nvim/init.lua"
			code={`vim.lsp.config("squill", {
  cmd = { "squill", "language-server", "start" },
  filetypes = {
    "sql", "rust", "go", "python", "javascript", "javascriptreact",
    "typescript", "typescriptreact", "gleam", "cpp", "cs", "java",
    "kotlin", "swift",
  },
  root_markers = { "squill.toml", ".config/squill.toml", ".git" },
})
vim.lsp.enable("squill")`}
		/>
		<p>
			Neovim layers the semantic tokens of every server attached to a buffer, so
			the SQL in a Rust file&rsquo;s strings is highlighted on top of
			rust-analyzer&rsquo;s highlighting. Formatting runs every attached
			server&rsquo;s formatter in turn; to run just squill&rsquo;s, name it:
		</p>
		<Code lang="lua" code={`vim.lsp.buf.format({ name = "squill" })`} />
	</section>
</Docs>
